<!-- doc/src/sgml/tableam.sgml -->

<chapter id="tableam">
 <title>Table Access Method Interface Definition</title>

 <indexterm>
  <primary>Table Access Method</primary>
 </indexterm>
 <indexterm>
  <primary>tableam</primary>
  <secondary>Table Access Method</secondary>
 </indexterm>

 <para>
  This chapter explains the interface between the core
  <productname>PostgreSQL</productname> system and <firstterm>table access
  methods</firstterm>, which manage the storage for tables. The core system
  knows little about these access methods beyond what is specified here, so
  it is possible to develop entirely new access method types by writing
  add-on code.
 </para>

 <para>
  Each table access method is described by a row in the <link
  linkend="catalog-pg-am"><structname>pg_am</structname></link> system
  catalog. The <structname>pg_am</structname> entry specifies a name and a
  <firstterm>handler function</firstterm> for the table access method.  These
  entries can be created and deleted using the <xref
  linkend="sql-create-access-method"/> and <xref
  linkend="sql-drop-access-method"/> SQL commands.
 </para>

 <para>
  A table access method handler function must be declared to accept a single
  argument of type <type>internal</type> and to return the pseudo-type
  <type>table_am_handler</type>.  The argument is a dummy value that simply
  serves to prevent handler functions from being called directly from SQL commands.
 </para>

 <para>
  Here is how an extension SQL script file might create a table access
  method handler:
 </para>

<programlisting>
CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION my_tableam_handler(internal)
  RETURNS table_am_handler AS 'my_extension', 'my_tableam_handler'
  LANGUAGE C STRICT;

CREATE ACCESS METHOD myam TYPE TABLE HANDLER my_tableam_handler;
</programlisting>

 <para>
  The result of the function must be a pointer to a struct of type
  <structname>TableAmRoutine</structname>, which contains everything that the
  core code needs to know to make use of the table access method. The return
  value needs to be of server lifetime, which is typically achieved by
  defining it as a <literal>static const</literal> variable in global scope.
 </para>

 <para>
  Here is how a source file with the table access method handler might
  look like:
 </para>

<programlisting><![CDATA[
#include "postgres.h"

#include "access/tableam.h"
#include "fmgr.h"

PG_MODULE_MAGIC;

static const TableAmRoutine my_tableam_methods = {
    .type = T_TableAmRoutine,

    /* Methods of TableAmRoutine omitted from example, add them here. */
};

PG_FUNCTION_INFO_V1(my_tableam_handler);

Datum
my_tableam_handler(PG_FUNCTION_ARGS)
{
    PG_RETURN_POINTER(&my_tableam_methods);
}
]]>
</programlisting>

 <para>
  The <structname>TableAmRoutine</structname> struct, also called the
  access method's <firstterm>API struct</firstterm>, defines the behavior of
  the access method using callbacks. These callbacks are pointers to plain C
  functions and are not visible or callable at the SQL level. All the
  callbacks and their behavior is defined in the
  <structname>TableAmRoutine</structname> structure (with comments inside the
  struct defining the requirements for callbacks). Most callbacks have
  wrapper functions, which are documented from the point of view of a user
  (rather than an implementor) of the table access method.  For details,
  please refer to the <ulink url="https://git.postgresql.org/gitweb/?p=postgresql.git;a=blob;f=src/include/access/tableam.h;hb=HEAD">
  <filename>src/include/access/tableam.h</filename></ulink> file.
 </para>

 <para>
  Tuple update, delete, and lock callbacks report failure facts through
  <structname>TM_FailureData</structname>, rather than exposing physical tuple
  headers.  For <literal>TM_SelfModified</literal>,
  <structfield>modified_by_same_command</structfield> compares the responsible
  command with the callback's <parameter>cid</parameter> argument, and
  <structfield>modified_by_current_transaction</structfield> includes current
  subtransactions.  When locking a later version, the responsible command can
  be the one that created that version.  For <literal>TM_Updated</literal>,
  <structfield>moved_partitions</structfield> identifies an update that moved
  the row to another partition.  These fields are false for other results.
 </para>

 <para>
  Every callback call must initialize all fields of
  <structname>TM_FailureData</structname>.  The failure facts describe the final
  result, not an intermediate retry.  The <structfield>retargeted</structfield>
  flag instead accumulates across retries within a single tuple lock call:
  it records that the call followed a later row version, even if locking
  ultimately failed.  On success, it requires the caller to recheck the row,
  and the returned locator identifies the locked version.  Retargeting need
  not change the locator for access methods that update rows in place.
 </para>

 <para>
  To implement an access method, an implementor will typically need to
  implement an <acronym>AM</acronym>-specific type of tuple table slot (see
  <ulink url="https://git.postgresql.org/gitweb/?p=postgresql.git;a=blob;f=src/include/executor/tuptable.h;hb=HEAD">
   <filename>src/include/executor/tuptable.h</filename></ulink>), which allows
   code outside the access method to hold references to tuples of the AM, and
   to access the columns of the tuple.
 </para>

 <para>
  Currently, the way an AM actually stores data is fairly unconstrained. For
  example, it's possible, but not required, to use postgres' shared buffer
  cache.  In case it is used, it likely makes sense to use
  <productname>PostgreSQL</productname>'s standard page layout as described in
  <xref linkend="storage-page-layout"/>.
 </para>

 <para>
  One fairly large constraint of the table access method API is that,
  currently, if the AM wants to support modifications and/or indexes, it is
  necessary for each tuple to have a tuple identifier (<acronym>TID</acronym>)
  consisting of a block number and an item number (see also <xref
  linkend="storage-page-layout"/>).  It is not strictly necessary that the
  sub-parts of <acronym>TIDs</acronym> have the same meaning they e.g., have
  for <literal>heap</literal>, but if bitmap scan support is desired (it is
  optional), the block number needs to provide locality.
 </para>

 <para>
  For crash safety, an AM can use postgres' <link
  linkend="wal"><acronym>WAL</acronym></link>, or a custom implementation.
  If <acronym>WAL</acronym> is chosen, either <link
  linkend="generic-wal">Generic WAL Records</link> can be used,
  or a <link linkend="custom-rmgr">Custom WAL Resource Manager</link> can be
  implemented.
 </para>

 <para>
  To implement transactional support in a manner that allows different table
  access methods be accessed within a single transaction, it likely is
  necessary to closely integrate with the machinery in
  <filename>src/backend/access/transam/xlog.c</filename>.
 </para>

 <para>
  Any developer of a new <literal>table access method</literal> can refer to
  the existing <literal>heap</literal> implementation present in
  <filename>src/backend/access/heap/heapam_handler.c</filename> for details of
  its implementation.
 </para>

 <para>
  A table access method can choose the code that implements each new index
  on its tables with the optional <function>index_implementation</function>
  callback.  It is given the table and the index's access method, and
  returns the OID of an index access method that implements indexes of that
  access method (see <xref linkend="index-implementations"/>), or
  <literal>InvalidOid</literal> for no preference, in which case
  <xref linkend="guc-default-btree-implementation"/> applies.  This lets a
  table access method use, for example, a <literal>btree</literal>
  implementation that stores versioned entries for its tables, while the
  indexes stay ordinary <literal>btree</literal> indexes to the planner and
  to users.  The choice is recorded when the index is created and is not
  revisited, so it survives rebuilds, dump and restore with the same table
  access method, and changes to the table's access method; it is not
  consulted for indexes on system catalogs or TOAST tables, nor while
  <application>pg_upgrade</application> restores a dump, which states every
  index's implementation itself.
 </para>

 <sect1 id="tableam-locator">
  <title>Row Locators</title>

  <para>
   The value an index stores to identify a row, and hands back to the table
   access method to fetch it, is the row's <firstterm>locator</firstterm>.
   For <literal>heap</literal> it is the <acronym>TID</acronym>.  An access
   method describes its locator with a <structname>LocatorDesc</structname>,
   returned by the <function>relation_locator</function> callback for each
   relation.  An access method that leaves the callback
   <literal>NULL</literal> is treated as having a locator like
   <literal>heap</literal>'s.  The fields are:
  </para>

  <variablelist>
   <varlistentry>
    <term><structfield>width</structfield></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      The locator's size in bytes; a negative value is the largest size of a
      variable-width locator.  An index access method stores a locator as a
      <acronym>TID</acronym> unless it sets
      <structfield>amcanvarlocator</structfield> (reported by
      <function>pg_indexam_has_property</function> as
      <literal>can_var_locator</literal>), so it can be used only on a table
      whose locator is fixed-width and no wider than 6 bytes; otherwise
      <command>CREATE INDEX</command>, and any command that rebuilds the
      table's indexes, fails.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><structfield>max_offset</structfield></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      The largest item number any locator carries.  If it exceeds what a
      bitmap can hold (<symbol>MaxHeapTuplesPerPage</symbol>), the planner
      does not consider bitmap scans of the table, and
      <acronym>GIN</acronym> indexes cannot be built on it.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><structfield>name</structfield></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      A name for the locator, used in error messages.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><structfield>stable</structfield></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Whether a row keeps its locator for its whole life.  For
      <literal>heap</literal> it does not: an update stores the new version
      at a new <acronym>TID</acronym>.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><structfield>old_version_retained</structfield></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Whether the version of a row that an <command>UPDATE</command> replaced
      can still be fetched by its locator.  If not, the executor copies the
      old row, and the new row for user row triggers, before they are needed,
      and the access method must keep a deleted row fetchable by its locator
      until the deleting transaction ends.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><structfield>versioned</structfield></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Whether an index entry names a row version as a locator and a 4-byte
      <firstterm>version tag</firstterm>, rather than by the locator alone.
      For <literal>heap</literal> it does not: an update that changes an
      indexed column gives the new version a new <acronym>TID</acronym>, and
      no entry carries a tag.  An access method that updates rows in place
      can say it does, and so keep a row's locator across an update of
      indexed columns; see <xref linkend="tableam-versioned-locators"/>.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><structfield>bitmap_and_inexact</structfield>, <structfield>bitmap_or_inexact</structfield></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Optional per-block callbacks that let the access method tell a
      <literal>BitmapAnd</literal> or <literal>BitmapOr</literal> that a
      block's item numbers cannot be combined exactly; the block is then
      combined conservatively and rechecked.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>
  </variablelist>

  <para>
   When the executor locks, updates or deletes a row it has already locked
   in the same command, it passes <symbol>TUPLE_LOCK_FLAG_LOCKED_VERSION</symbol>,
   <symbol>TABLE_UPDATE_LOCKED_VERSION</symbol> or
   <symbol>TABLE_DELETE_LOCKED_VERSION</symbol>.  An access method whose
   locator names a row rather than a version uses these to tell which
   version the caller means; see <filename>src/include/access/tableam.h</filename>.
  </para>

  <para>
   <filename>src/include/access/amlocator.h</filename> describes each field
   in detail.  Core code also relies on these properties of every locator:
  </para>

  <itemizedlist>
   <listitem>
    <para>
     The locator is the row's <structfield>ctid</structfield>.  It identifies
     the target row of <command>UPDATE</command>, <command>DELETE</command>,
     <command>MERGE</command>, <literal>SELECT FOR UPDATE</literal> and
     <literal>WHERE CURRENT OF</literal>, and is what <acronym>TID</acronym>
     scans look up.
    </para>
   </listitem>
   <listitem>
    <para>
     An item number of zero or greater than <symbol>MaxOffsetNumber</symbol>,
     and the block number <symbol>InvalidBlockNumber</symbol>, are reserved
     for other uses and must not appear in a locator.
    </para>
   </listitem>
   <listitem>
    <para>
     Locators are ordered by block number, then item number, and rows that
     are close in that order should be close in storage.
     <acronym>B-tree</acronym> indexes order duplicate keys by locator,
     <command>ANALYZE</command> sorts its sample by it, <acronym>TID</acronym>
     range scans return rows in that order, and <acronym>BRIN</acronym>
     indexes summarize ranges of block numbers.
    </para>
   </listitem>
  </itemizedlist>

  <sect2 id="tableam-versioned-locators">
   <title>Versioned Locators</title>

   <para>
    An index entry for a table whose locator is
    <structfield>versioned</structfield> carries a
    <firstterm>version tag</firstterm>, of type
    <type>IndexVersionTag</type> (a 4-byte unsigned integer).  The contract
    between the table access method, index access methods and core code is:
   </para>

   <itemizedlist>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Tag 0 (<symbol>InvalidIndexVersionTag</symbol>) means
      <quote>untagged</quote>: the entry was made by an access method that
      does not tag, or for a row version older than the oldest one the table
      access method keeps history for.  It does not mean <quote>the current
      version</quote>.
     </para>
    </listitem>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      The table access method hands out tags and alone defines what they
      mean.  Only it can tell whether an entry (key, locator, tag) is valid
      for the version of the row a snapshot sees, and when old tags may be
      forgotten.
     </para>
    </listitem>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      An index access method that sets
      <structfield>amcanversion</structfield> stores, orders and returns tags,
      but never interprets them (see <xref linkend="index-api"/>).  Other
      index access methods store no tags.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </itemizedlist>

   <para>
    When the executor updates a row of such a table, it works out which
    indexed attributes the update changes.  If every non-summarizing index
    whose attributes (as computed by
    <function>RelationGetIndexedAttrs</function>: key and included columns,
    and the columns its expressions and predicate reference) overlap them
    can store tags and is ready for inserts, it passes
    <symbol>TABLE_UPDATE_MAY_KEEP_LOCATOR</symbol> to
    <function>tuple_update</function>.  The access method may then keep the
    row's locator.  It reports the outcome in
    <literal>*update_indexes</literal>, and in
    <literal>*version_tag</literal> the tag of the new version, which every
    entry the executor makes for that version carries:
   </para>

   <variablelist>
    <varlistentry>
     <term><literal>TU_Affected</literal></term>
     <listitem>
      <para>
       The row kept its locator, and the indexed attributes the executor
       passed as <literal>modified_idx_attrs</literal> changed.  Every
       non-summarizing index whose attributes overlap them, and every
       summarizing index, gets an entry carrying
       <literal>*version_tag</literal>, which must not be 0; every other
       index keeps its entries.  Only an access method whose locator is
       versioned, given <symbol>TABLE_UPDATE_MAY_KEEP_LOCATOR</symbol>, may
       report this.
      </para>
     </listitem>
    </varlistentry>
    <varlistentry>
     <term><literal>TU_All</literal>, <literal>TU_Summarizing</literal>, <literal>TU_None</literal></term>
     <listitem>
      <para>
       As for any access method: every index, only the summarizing ones, or
       none get entries.  With <literal>TU_All</literal> the row may have
       moved, and <literal>*version_tag</literal> may be 0 or not, as the
       access method chooses: a moved row has no history at its new locator.
      </para>
     </listitem>
    </varlistentry>
   </variablelist>

   <para>
    The same rule applies to logical replication apply and to
    <command>REPACK (CONCURRENTLY)</command>.
   </para>

   <para>
    Entries made for a new row (by <command>INSERT</command> or
    <command>COPY</command>) are untagged; a new row has no history, so that
    is valid.  Tags come only from the table access method: with the new
    versions <function>tuple_update</function> makes, and from its index
    build and validation scans, which set
    <structfield>ii_VersionTag</structfield> in the
    <structname>IndexInfo</structname> before handing each row to the index.
   </para>

   <para>
    The table access method's obligations are:
   </para>

   <itemizedlist>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      For an entry of an index whose access method sets
      <structfield>amcanversion</structfield>, decide whether it is valid for
      the version of the row a snapshot sees, that is, whether its key is
      that version's key.  The rule this interface was designed for: the
      entry is valid if its tag names that version or an earlier one, and no
      version since then changed an attribute the index reads; an untagged
      entry counts as naming the oldest version the access method keeps
      history for.  An entry of any other index is resolved by locator
      alone: the executor never lets a row keep its locator across an update
      that affects such an index, so its entries describe every version
      their locator reaches, as with <literal>heap</literal>.  Every caller
      that hands an entry to the table access method also identifies the
      index.
     </para>
    </listitem>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      When building or validating an <structfield>amcanversion</structfield>
      index, tag a row whose history changed an attribute the index reads
      with the current version's tag; leave it untagged only if there is no
      such history.  Otherwise the new entry would be judged older than that
      history, and rejected.
     </para>
    </listitem>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      When building any index from a row's current version while an older
      version with different values for the index may still be visible to
      some transaction, set <structfield>ii_BrokenHotChain</structfield>, as
      <literal>heap</literal> does for a broken <acronym>HOT</acronym> chain,
      so that such transactions do not use the index.
     </para>
    </listitem>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Keep enough history to decide validity for every entry any index still
      holds, and discard history only once no entry depends on it.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </itemizedlist>

   <para>
    An access method whose locator is versioned must provide the
    <function>fetch_tid_version</function> callback, which a unique check
    uses instead of <function>fetch_tid</function>: given an entry's
    locator, tag and index, it reports whether a version of the row for
    which that entry is valid is visible to the snapshot, and whether no
    snapshot needs the entry any more.
   </para>

   <para>
    The <acronym>TID</acronym> bitmap of a bitmap scan holds locators without
    tags, so <function>scan_bitmap_next_tuple</function> must ask for a recheck
    of any row whose locator a bitmap may hold only through an entry that is
    not valid for the visible version.  Plain and index-only scans need
    nothing beyond the validity rule: the table access method's
    <structfield>xs_getnext_slot</structfield> callback sees the entry's tag
    in <structfield>xs_heaptag</structfield>.
   </para>
  </sect2>
 </sect1>

</chapter>
